{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WRmy8LTbPuwQgUnbuyABXX","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→{\n     2→  \"metadata\": {\n     3→    \"title\": \"The Cask of Amontillado - Poe\",\n     4→    \"author\": \"Edgar Allan Poe\",\n     5→    \"source_file\": \"Test_Texts/The Cask of Amontillado - Poe.txt\",\n     6→    \"source_format\": \"txt\",\n     7→    \"total_word_count\": 2358,\n     8→    \"total_character_count\": 13023,\n     9→    \"estimated_total_duration_minutes\": 15.693333333333333,\n    10→    \"words_per_minute\": 150\n    11→  },\n    12→  \"structure\": [\n    13→    {\n    14→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    15→      \"title\": null,\n    16→      \"index\": 1,\n    17→      \"start_position\": 24,\n    18→      \"end_position\": 13023,\n    19→      \"word_count\": 2354,\n    20→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 15.693333333333333,\n    21→      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n    22→      \"children\": [],\n    23→      \"characters_present\": [\n    24→        \"Montresor\",\n    25→        \"Fortunato\"\n    26→      ],\n    27→      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with the narrator, Montresor, vowing revenge against Fortunato for an unspecified insult, resolving to punish him with impunity during the chaotic carnival season. At dusk, Montresor lures the drunken, costumed Fortunato—wearing a jingling motley outfit and conical cap—through the damp, bone-strewn catacombs beneath his palazzo by feigning concern over a pipe of supposed Amontillado and invoking the rival wine expert Luchresi. As they descend deeper into the vaults, Montresor offers wine to mask the rising nitre and feigns concern for Fortunato’s cough, all while subtly guiding him toward a hidden niche. Once there, Montresor chains Fortunato to the wall with a padlock and begins sealing him alive behind a stone wall, layer by layer, as Fortunato’s initial laughter turns to desperate pleas and finally silence. Montresor completes the wall, reassembles the original bone pile, and leaves the body undisturbed for fifty years.\"\n    28→    }\n    29→  ],\n    30→  \"characters\": [\n    31→    {\n    32→      \"id\": \"char_amontillado_1b7d65f7\",\n    33→      \"canonical_name\": \"Amontillado\",\n    34→      \"aliases\": [],\n    35→      \"role\": null,\n    36→      \"appearance\": null,\n    37→      \"personality\": null,\n    38→      \"voice_guidance\": null,\n    39→      \"descriptions\": [\n    40→        {\n    41→          \"text\": \"The character is the first-person narrator of the story and serves as the storyteller. They express doubt about the authenticity of a pipe of Amontillado and state they paid the full price without consulting Fortunato. The narrator repeatedly refers to 'Amontillado' as a wine, and insists on verifying its authenticity by seeking a critical opinion from Luchresi. The narrator guides Fortunato through a crypt to reach the Amontillado, and at the end, they humorously confirm the name of the wine as a joke, suggesting a tone of irony and self-awareness. The narrator's role is central to the narrative structure, as they recount events and maintain a consistent voice throughout the story.\",\n    42→          \"source_position\": 2046,\n    43→          \"chapter_index\": null,\n    44→          \"confidence\": \"llm_refined\"\n    45→        }\n    46→      ],\n    47→      \"relationships\": {},\n    48→      \"evidence\": [\n    49→        {\n    50→          \"statement\": \"The narrator expresses doubt about the authenticity of a pipe of Amontillado\",\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc752f08-a97e-414a-80a9-5